2025-01-25 13:53:14,329 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': 600, 'files': None, 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': '\n\nHuman: Please grade the following student solution based on the exercise description and grading rubric provided.\n\nExercise Description:\nExecise instructions I write these lines to provide some advise and ease your minds to undertake the exam for the subject ‘Consumer Market Research’.  The exam is based on the cooperative Design Sprint exercise we undertook at class by teams. Specifically it will focus on the last stage which is the Customer Interview. For such you will have to execute one, two or more interviews, preferably the latter. In this case, the interviews can be done by different team members. The interviews must be either (remotely) simultaneously witnessed by all the other teams members. Or, the interview is recorded and shared. Once the interview is analyses the team should have a joint session to share their observations. The exam deliverable is a document written with any word processor with a minimum extensino of 1 page, not including front page if it exists. It is recommend including a front page with the project title and the name of the memebers in the team. It should answer the following topics: How did you prepare the environment for the interviews?  AHow did you prepare the environment for the team analysis of the observations? Which were you difficulties for finding interviews?  What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? Which were the observations that generated the mos discussion or took the longest time?\n\nGrading Rubric:\nHere are the rules and heuristics applied for grading the different categories. The criteria follow a consistent structure and syntax to ensure clarity and precision. <Category> Clarity </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is exceptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nMOANA INTERVIEW :  In order to end the project of design sprint we had the opportunity to conduct an interview where we were able to understand if our product could be interesting for customers. We conducted an interview with Robbe, a friend of one the team members that is currently studying in Portugal and surfing on average twice a week. The following document will answer the questions demanded for this project. How was the environment prepared for the interview? The interview was conducted online, with only two team members present during the call to avoid overwhelming the interviewee. The session began with a friendly and informal conversation, allowing the interviewee to feel at ease and establish a connection with the interviewer. This approach helped create a safe and relaxed environment, encouraging the interviewee to share openly. The interviewee was a friend of one of our teammates, Flynn, which further contributed to a comfortable dynamic and made it easier to build trust. Additionally, the team ensured that technical aspects, such as a stable internet connection and clear audio, were in place to facilitate smooth communication.  How was the team analysis conducted for the observation? To ensure a smooth and structured interview, we prepared a detailed document in advance, dividing it into three key sections. The first section focused on discussing general topics to set the stage. The second section delved into understanding the specific challenges he faces within the sport of surfing. Finally, we presented him with a visualized concept of our products, including mockup designs and descriptions of their intended functionality. We then asked for his feedback and perspective on the proposed products. The interview was video recorded, enabling the entire team to review and analyze the session afterward. This approach ensured that all team members could contribute to the insights, even if they weren’t present during the live interview. During the call, the two team members present focused on active listening and observing the interviewee\'s body language and tone, which provided valuable non-verbal cues. Notes were taken individually, focusing on key behaviors, emotional responses, and the specific content of the answers. By combining the video review and personal notes, the team could cross-reference observations and identify patterns, themes, and unique perspectives that might have been overlooked during the live session. Finally, the whole team had a team meeting in order to discuss and share analyses about the interview.  What were the main challenges encountered in finding interviewees? The primary challenge was locating a professional surfer who fit the project\'s target audience. To address this, the team opted for an experienced amateur surfer, which still aligned with the project goals. Personal connections were crucial, as relying on a friend ensured the process moved forward. Without this connection, finding a suitable candidate who matched the values of the project would have been significantly more time-consuming and difficult. This challenge highlighted the importance of having a strong network and the potential need to expand outreach strategies for future interviews.   What strategies could facilitate reaching five interviews?  Several strategies could be employed to secure additional interviews:   Social media : Platforms like Instagram or Facebook can be used to connect with surfers or communities interested in the sport. Creating posts or ads targeted at these audiences might yield quick responses.   Using already existing network : Asking current interviewees to recommend friends or acquaintances who meet the project criteria can expand the pool of candidates organically.   Contact surfing schools : Directly contacting local surf schools or clubs to request interviews could provide access to a variety of potential interviewees, from beginners to professionals.   Event participation :  Attending  a surfing event or competitions could provide an opportunity to network and get in touch with potential interviewees.   What were the most surprising insights from the interviews? Water Intrusion in the wetsuit : In fact water intrusion into the wetsuit was a significant concern, but the interviewee mentioned that as long as the wetsuit was fully zipped and snug, he wasn’t worried. However, when detachable sections, such as the arms, were unzipped, he expressed concerns about water entering.   Rental wetsuit frustrations: Renting wetsuits posed multiple challenges, including improper fit, unpleasant smells from previous users, and occasionally damp conditions. These issues impacted the overall surfing experience and highlighted the value of customization and quality in wetsuit design.   Tight fit for warmth: The interviewee stressed that a snug wetsuit was essential for warmth and comfort, underscoring the importance of designing suits that balance ease of use with effective insulation.   Preference for modularity:  The concept of detachable sections, allowing for easy replacement of damaged parts, was particularly interesting to his eyes. It aligned with the interviewee\'s desire for practicality and sustainability.   Which observations generated the most discussion or took the longest time?  Water intrusion : The challenge of preventing water from entering the wetsuit, especially with modular designs, raised questions about sealing techniques and material innovations.   Difficulty in putting on wetsuits: The effort required to put on a tight-fitting wetsuit, especially when it was damp, was a recurring issue that resonated within the discussion.  Rental wetsuit issues : The drawbacks of renting wetsuits, such as improper fit and hygiene concerns, highlighted the need for a more user-friendly rental system or improved wetsuit designs.   How can one prepare to effectively utilize the interview technique in projects, initiatives, or jobs? Additionally, what improvements could be made to gain deeper insights and maximize learning from the interview process?  The interview technique is a powerful tool for understanding consumer needs and aligning project designs with real-world expectations. Preparation is key to maximizing its effectiveness. As someone aspiring to create my own company, I recognize the importance of interviews in identifying consumer pain points and refining product ideas. I have personally applied interview techniques during entrepreneurship competitions, and I found them to be incredibly valuable. When you have an idea to solve someone\'s problem, it is crucial to thoroughly understand the problem itself and how consumers are experiencing it. These insights ensure that your solution truly addresses their needs.   To improve the interviews that we did I would improve the following points :  - Conduct multiple interviews with diverse participants to gather a broad range of perspectives. Because you can focus your whole analysis of your product just on one interview or consumer.  - Use technology, such as transcription tools, to analyze responses systematically and identify trends.   - Encourage interviewees to share anecdotes or specific experiences, which often reveal valuable details.   Finally I think the main points that I would remember are the following. Firstly it is important to define clear goals. In fact establishing specific objectives for the interview ensures that the questions are focused and the insights gathered are actionable. Secondly, do some research. Understanding the context and background of the interviewee allows for more personalized and relevant questions, fostering deeper engagement. It would be nice to create a structured yet flexible approach. Indeed, preparing a mix of open-ended and targeted questions ensures that the interview covers key topics while allowing for organic exploration of unexpected insights. Finally, repeating the same questions in different ways encourages the interviewee to reflect more deeply on the matter and provide interesting answers, as observed in this project.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
