ews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nHow We Prepared for the Interviews and Feedback Analysis To keep things consistent, we followed a structured approach for the interviews, including:  A friendly introduction to set the tone. Asking contextual questions to understand the participant’s surfing habits. Presenting the wetsuit prototype virtually. Wrapping up with a discussion to gather detailed feedback. The interviews were held online via Microsoft Teams, where we used screen sharing to demonstrate the prototype. Before each interview, we made sure participants understood the process and gave their consent to be recorded. After the interviews, we analyzed the feedback by organizing it into categories using an observation matrix. Our team also met after each session to talk through the findings and ensure everyone was on the same page about the insights. Challenges in Finding Participants and How We Could Do Better What made it hard: Finding experienced surfers who could provide valuable feedback was tougher than we expected. Since we relied mainly on personal connections, the pool of participants was limited. Scheduling conflicts made it tricky to find a time that worked for everyone. What we could do next time: Reach out to surfing groups at universities, local surf clubs, and shops. Use social media to promote the project and attract more diverse participants. Offer flexible time slots, like evenings or weekends, to accommodate busy schedules. What Surprised Us the Most Some feedback really stood out, such as concerns about how the product might affect performance:  “I’d worry about the cameras affecting the flexibility of the wetsuit or being damaged in rough conditions.” “If the camera adds too much weight, it could make paddling more difficult.” Durability came up repeatedly:  “I’m not sure how well the cameras would hold up in big waves or crashes.” Ease of use also sparked some scepticism:  “It seems like a good idea, but I’m not sure it would be easy enough to use during a real session.” These comments highlighted the need to balance innovation with practicality, as surfers prioritize simplicity and reliability in their gear. What Took the Longest to Discuss Camera placement: There was a lot of excitement about adding a back camera:  “Having a camera on the back to capture 340° footage sounds fantastic.” But we debated how practical it would be without affecting movement. Durability: Comments like “I’d worry about the cameras being damaged in big waves” prompted discussions about how to make the design more robust. Removable vs. built-in cameras: Some participants liked the idea of removable cameras for upgrades:  “That way, I wouldn’t need to buy an entirely new wetsuit if I want better cameras.” But we also talked about how this might complicate the design or increase costs. Battery life and usability: Questions about battery duration and charging options came up often and led to ideas for improvement, as surfers would want the product to last throughout long sessions. Preparing for Interviews in Projects, Initiatives, or Jobs: Understand the Purpose: Before beginning any interview, we make sure to understand the core objective of the interview. We will identify whether the goal is to validate a concept, discover user needs, or gather feedback on a prototype. Clear objectives help guide the types of questions we’ll ask. Plan and Structure the Interview: We’ll adopt a structured approach by preparing a well-defined interview guide. This would include: Broad opening questions to make the interviewee comfortable and build rapport. Contextual questions that help us understand the user’s background, habits, and challenges. Questions about their interaction with the prototype or project, and how they might use it in their daily lives. Clear instructions about the prototype’s features and purpose to ensure participants can engage effectively. Choose the Right Tools: Based on the professor’s recommendations, we will: Use Zoom/Teams for video interviews with features like screen-sharing, which allow us to present prototypes easily. Use Miro/Mural for collaborative note-taking and capturing real-time feedback from team members during the interview. Record the session for later analysis, using tools like Loom or Clipchamp, to capture the interviewee’s behaviour and verbal feedback. Team Roles: We would establish roles for the interview team, including a primary interviewer, note-takers, and technical support. Each team member should have a clear role to avoid confusion and ensure smooth data capture. Test and Troubleshoot the Tools Beforehand: We’ll conduct technical checks, test recording features, and ensure everyone understands how to use the software before the interviews start. This will help avoid any delays during the interviews. Improving Learning from Interviews: Adapt to Interviewee Behaviour: We will remain flexible and adjust our interview techniques based on the interviewee’s responses and body language. For example, if someone seems confused or hesitant, we might need to ask more clarifying questions. Refine Questioning Techniques: We will be mindful not to ask leading or biased questions. Instead, we will ask open-ended questions that encourage the interviewee to share their thoughts and experiences freely. To dig deeper, we can use techniques like “Why?” or “Can you elaborate on that?” Encourage Think-Aloud Protocols: Encouraging participants to \\"think aloud\\" as they interact with prototypes or tools can help us better understand their thought processes and decision-making. This will provide more valuable insights into their mental models. Use Multiple Data Sources: We will cross-check and complement interview data with other methods such as user surveys, observational data, or analytics to gain a more comprehensive understanding of the user experience. Follow-up Interviews or Clarifications: We may schedule follow-up interviews to clarify any uncertainties or deepen our understanding of specific feedback. Sometimes, a single interview may not reveal everything, and additional context may be required to extract meaningful insights. Improve Observation Skills: While verbal feedback is essential, non-verbal cues such as body language, facial expressions, and tone of voice can provide deeper insights. We will pay special attention to these cues and consider them when analysing data. Post-Interview Debriefs: After every interview, we will conduct a debrief session with the team to discuss key findings, notable comments, and emerging patterns. This collaborative analysis will help us generate actionable insights more effectively.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
