ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nDESIGN SPRINT – FINAL EXAM Giacomo Galliussi    INTERVIEW INTERPRETATION  How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? And, the same for the team analysis of  the observations?   We decided to conduct two interviews to experts that work in the football industry. We chose two  contacts that I had from my previous internship experiences at Watford FC and at Udinese Calcio. For  Watford FC we had the possibility to interview Mr. Gino Pozzo who is the owner of the club and works  even as co-owner at Udinese Calcio which is his father’s club since 1986. Instead for Udinese Calcio  we interviewed Mr. Oliver Grice-Hewitt who is the grounds manager at the club. He can be considered  more of an expert than Mr. Pozzo because he is closer to the ﬁeld of our product and for this reason it  was probably even the most valuable interview out of the two.   To prepare the environment for the interviews, ﬁrst of all I contacted via e-mail the two experts hoping  they would remember me from my past internships. After I asked them if it was possible to interview  them about our design sprint storyboard which was about a robot that was cable of restoring the ﬁeld  at half-time without the presence of groundsman. Both of them sounded really interested in this idea  and agreed to have a talk with our “company” Patch & Play. We set an appointment with them on Zoom  on two separate days so that we had enough time to prepare for both interviews. As for the environment,  we chose a space with a professional background that was well-lit and obviously that was very silent.  Before each interview we performed all the routine tests such as camera, microphone, and internet  connection. I personally carried out all the interviews since I was the reference person from the group  while Paolo was taking notes, and another person was checking all the technical problems. The rest of  the team was observing and carefully listening to the interview. Moreover, to make the guests feel more  comfortable I started the interviews with a friendly welcome and after I went on asking some contextual  questions to introduce the topic.   As for the environment for the team analysis of the observations, we decided to do a preliminary meeting  in-person some days after the interviews were carried out so that it was easier to discuss the main  points from the interviews and to decide what the next steps would be. In this case we met at one of the  group member’s house in a quiet environment. Before starting with the discussion, we had a welcome  chat just joking and talking about everyday topics so that everyone felt comfortable. This aspect is really  important even for our future careers because if you want a positive outcome form a meeting you should  never start by speaking about the meeting topic, but you should do a sort of “warm up round of  questions”. After the in-person discussion, we organized what each member had to do to analyse the  interview and then we started working on the matrixes which were completed by everyone based on the  role they had in the company. We used tools like whiteboards, shared documents, and digital boards to  organize ideas and generate solutions. During this phase we organized a lot of online meetings to share  updates, feedbacks and any doubts, for example on the interpretation of some questions. Even the  online meetings were carried out with a preliminary welcome talk to create a positive environment.     Which were your diƯiculties in ﬁnding interviews? What would you consider would help to reach  5 interviewees?   In ﬁnding people to interview for this speciﬁc project we didn’t have many problems because it was easy  for me to ﬁnd two experts that I knew in the football industry and with whom I had spent some time in  the past summers. But I must say that if the task was to ﬁnd 5 interviewees it wouldn’t have been so  easy because I wouldn’t have had as many contacts to ask to. So as for the di Ưiculties that might arise  in ﬁnding interviewees, the ﬁrst one is that you might not have enough contacts. The solution to this  problem is ﬁrst of all to always do networking at events and in work situations because you never know  what you will need in the future. Secondly, if your network isn’t that good you can always try and contact  people on career websites, like LinkedIn, where experts are usually more than happy in helping. Another  diƯiculty might be related to time constraints because especially in this case we had very little time, due  even to the holiday period, to contact, schedule and conduct interviews but we were fortunate because  our experts were available with short notice. For this di Ưiculty there isn’t a great solution, you should  just start working ahead because it might take time to arrange appointments and get answers from  experts. Another challenge might be connected to specialization of experts. This is m aybe a di Ưiculty  we faced partially, in fact we managed to ﬁnd both interviewees from the football world but, in my  opinion, the one who really ﬁtted in perfectly with what we were looking for was the grounds manager of  Udinese Calcio because he had greater knowledge on the topic. So, ﬁnding professionals whose  expertise directly aligns with your prototype may limit the pool of potential interviewees and the solution  in this case is to join specialized forums, groups, or communities related to ﬁeld recovery or robotics.    What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?   The most suppressive comments that emerged from both the interviews were regarding reliability and  durability, adaptability, training and usability. As for reliability and durability both Mr. Gino Pozzo and Mr.  Oliver Grice-Hewitt had concerns about how the robot would perform under challenging conditions like  adverse weather and how long would the robot last if it had to perform always in harsh weather which is  something typical of Watford and London. The second topic of concern was the one regarding  adaptability of our robot because we were questioned how well the robot adapts to diverse ﬁeld  conditions and it’s capability to function across varying environments. As for training and usability both  our experts pointed out the importance of ensuring the robot is easy to use and integrates seamlessly  with existing teams of groundsman. I must say that regarding this point especially Mr. Oliver was  reluctant because he illustrated that with the robot many groundsmen are going to lose their jobs, but  what we explained to him is that the robot is meant to collaborate with groundsmen and not to steal  their jobs. Furthermore, the robot is only usable at half-time for quick ﬁeld recovery but it still can’t give  the same results as groundsmen work does throughout the week before the match.   As for the storyboard that was shown to the interviewees, both of them sought clariﬁcation on speciﬁc  steps, especially regarding the purpose of meetings and training. For example, for Step 3 Mr. Gino Pozzo  sought clariﬁcation on whether the meeting is informative or sales-focused. Other concerns arose  regarding whether the robot could complete tasks within the given timeframe and how well it would  integrate into existing systems. For instance, for Step 5 we were asked how training for groundsmen will  be conducted. The last part of the storyboard that wasn’t 100% clear to our experts was the one  regarding evaluation metrics. More in particular, there was interest in understanding the data collected  and how it would validate the robot’s performance.    Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time?    The observations from our experts that generated the most discussion and that took up more time to be  explained were regarding the scalability of the robot and the robot maintenance. In fact, Mr. Gino Pozzo  asked: “How scalable is this solution? Are there plans to deploy it across multiple stadiums  simultaneously, and how would you ensure consistency in performance?\\" In this case the discussion  extended because scalability involves multiple aspects, such as software standardization, operational  logistics, and consistent performance across varied conditions. For this reason, we had to go back to  the storyboard and elaborate more on the technical and operational part of the robot which hadn’t been  explained well until we weren’t made this question by our expert.   Mr. Oliver Grice-Hewitt, grounds manager at Udinese Calcio, instead questioned: “How often does the  robot require maintenance, and is this something groundskeepers can manage?\\". This was something  which we hadn’t speciﬁed in our storyboard and for this reason the expert made an observation and had  doubts about it. Fortunately, we were able to give him a satisfactory answer because of the preliminary  research we had conducted but it obviously took a lot of time because we were asked follow-up  questions about real-world testing results or scenarios to validate these claims.    How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives or jobs.  And, what you consider improving to obtain more learning from the interviews.   I feel that that conducting these two interviews is going to be really helpful for my future, both studying  and in my career. First of all, because I have learnt how to approach an expert and what style of writing I  need to use in the ﬁrst contacts I make with him. In most cases it is fundamental to use a professional  style that makes the expert understand that you have knowledge on the topic, even though I must say  that in my case I could use a more relaxed style because I knew who I was contacting. Secondly, I have  understood that it is always important to carry out preliminary research before speaking with experts  because you need to know what you are talking about. Obviously even some research on the interviewee  should be conducted to be sure that the person ﬁts what you are looking for. Another interesting aspect  is that not always the experts interviewed share with you the information you were looking for, so at  times they might disappoint you. What I have learnt is that when this happens you need to be ready to  extrapolate only the useful information and make the most of it.   I believe I am going to use interviews even for future studies or projects because, although they take up  a lot of time to be prepared and conducted, I think they can give you really interesting insights on what  you are studying and a lot of information that is di Ưicult to ﬁnd in books or online . Furthermore, it is  always good exercise to get in contact with new people because in this way you can expand your network  which will certainly be helpful for the future career. What I wasn’t expecting is that before starting  interviews and meetings you should do an introduction and some welcome questions to make the  interviewees feel more comfortable. I was really interested in this approach and I will certainly keep it in  mind for the future because what I thought before this assignment is that, especially in the world of  work, professionals don’t have any time to waste and so they go straight to the point in meetings, instead  it is fundamental to have a warm up talk.    To obtain more learning from the interviews I would consider improving some aspect which I felt didn’t  work well in this case. One of these is to be more ﬂexible and curious because in my opinion during  these interviews I was too rigid (maybe because these were the ﬁrst ones of my life) and so once the  expert had answered to the question I quickly moved on to the next one even though the answer had  sparked a new line of thought that could be explored further. Another area of improvement is to spend  more time once the interview is ﬁnished to review notes and themes that might lead to unexpected  insights which you could forget if you did this activity the following day. Furthermore, what I would really  like to do is to ask interviewees how they felt during the interview to gain even more feedback, instead  in this case I only had feedback from my group members. On more important thing I would improve is to  change the questions you have prepared depending on who you have in front of you so that you can help  the interviewee to share more information on the topic and make you can make sure you are not asking  things he has already explained, but I think this is something that can be improved only over time and by  practicing a lot.  GROUPWORK & FINAL CONSIDERATIONS  This assignment was completed in groups made up of 6/7 people and this obviously had both some  positive aspects but at the same time even some drawbacks. As for the positive aspects it was extremely  important to have the possibility to choose our group members because there was a big amount of work  to be done in little time and so it was fundamental that group dynamics were already consolidated.  Consolidation can happen only by choosing people you know well both in private life but even as for the  way they participate in class. I had the fortune to work with people who had the same objectives as I had  and who wanted to bring out this assignment in the best possible way. I was extremely satisﬁed with the  fact that we worked in a serious manner and understood when it was time to take a pause and relax, and  when it was time to put all the e Ưort in the project. As for the e Ưort, many group members also worked  at home sometimes in order to come to the next class and be ready to follow the new instructions for  the assignment. This was really helpful especially for the Facilitator because it was easier to stay into  the time limits we had. As for the number of people that composed each group, I think it was correct  because in every task each member had something to do, and this was fundamental because there  wasn’t much time.   On the other hand, having so many di Ưerent personalities to deal with in so little time was certainly a  negative aspect. In fact, what we experienced in our group was that maybe there were too many strong  personalities, so too many leaders, and everyone wanted to say their opinion and try to convince the  rest of the group to follow it. Fortunately, in these situations there was the Decider who had the  possibility to decide and moderate between the di Ưerent ideas of each group member. As I stated  numerous times, the most di Ưicult thing to do was to follow the time that was allowed for each task and  I could personally feel the pressure connected to timing because I was the Facilitator.   As for what I would improve regarding the Design Sprint for the future, ﬁrst of all I would obviously give  more time to complete the di Ưerent tasks. I understand it is as “ sprint” as the title says but maybe in  many occasions we hadn’t enough time to do a great job in the single step of the assignment. The best  solution for this would be to have one or two lessons in addition to the four that we already had. The  other important aspect which I would change is to have all the lessons, one after the other, in a single  week because what happened this year is that we went to the study trip in Munich which was in between  the Design Sprint and this was absolutely terrible because we lost the focus on what we were doing. In  conclusion, I must admit that I really enjoyed this kind of assignment because it was realistic, and I  could understand the point of what I was doing. In my opinion, it was also explained well by the Professor  Juan Vazquez with clear and brief instructions for each step of the sprint.     FINAL CONSIDERATIONS & POSSIBLE IMPROVEMENTS ON LESSONS    As for the general lessons, in my opinion, there are some aspects that could be changed. First of all, I  believe that having some tangible examples of what we were doing would have been more helpful. As I  said to the Professor Juan Vazquez it would have been even better if these examples were related to  sports, but I understand it might have been di Ưicult to ﬁnd them. Furthermore, I think that it would have  been simpler to have a ﬁnal summary at the end of each lesson or before the groupwork that we had to  ﬁnish in class. Summaries and examples would have helped to clarify what kind of output was expected  from us in the assignments that were to be completed during class. The main aspect I would change is  to have always the same groups for assignments during the semester so that when it gets to the Design  Sprint the dynamics in the group have already been consolidated. In my case I didn’t have this problem  because I chose people I already knew well, but I understand that for other groups it might have been a  big issue that made them lose a lot of time especially at the beginning.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
