ews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nETHAN COLLINGS: How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? And the same for the team analysis of the observations? To prepare for the interview, we contacted the player in a professional manner and continued to organize with efficiency. As well as this, we held the interview in a nice office space and offered the player coffee, and either an apple or banana. We maintained a welcoming and friendly manner the entire time and made them feel secure.  For the team analysis, we all met up at the university to discuss the dialect we got from the interview. We all collected our opinions about the interview, and each had time to explain what we thought the best and worst points were. We then evaluated and gave opinions on each other’s opinions. Whilst maintaining a professional manner  Which were your difficulties in finding interviews? What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? What we found hardest for finding an interviewee was finding the specific target audience that we wanted. We were unable to find a professional within the industry due to our lack of time and ability to get in contact with people within this industry. Furthermore, due to the time of the year, it was hard to create time due to everyone being busy with family time at Christmas and New Year’s Eve.  To reach 5 interviewees:  We could have leveraged the use of social media more, such as LinkedIn, but also trying to tap into the industry using networks and emails. However, we were not resilient enough due to lack of time. Furthermore, we could have asked the amateur basketball player to gather more of his teammates, but we felt as if this would make the interview not as professional and contain the type of answers we were looking for.  What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? For me, the point where the interviewee mentioned the drainage system and the point in was important because it made us wonder if we needed it. It raised concerns and made us reevaluate the mechanism. I personally liked how the interviewee gave us something to think about from a different perspective. Additionally, we liked having a discussion with the interviewee as it saw us get passionate about the product, showing that we believe that the idea could potentially see success, even if it was not the actual idea, but the individual mechanisms.  Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time?  Again, for me, probably the same point as before, it really made us question our mechanisms of the project. Additionally, they mentioned the placement of the holes, and the size in general. This raised concerns again about our product and for me it made me think about the size of the holes and affecting the basketball.  In general, we all mentioned that the design may look simple and easy to understand, however the smaller individual parts such as the mechanisms would really sell the product more.  How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in the future? For me, I would research more around the industry to gain a better understanding of how it runs and increase my networking within the industry. I would also give myself more time to prepare for it. Furthermore, I would cut down on the personal questions at the start as I feel as if they were not needed, but I understand why you may need them in other scenarios.  Another thing I would try and improve is my knowledge in general about the topic and cause. This is because my actual knowledge of basketball courts and the mechanisms under the court were not complete, as I had never had an interest in this before. Therefore, I would research around my interview topic prior to the interview, to improve the quality of the questions and answers, giving us more informative content.  What do you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews? I would improve my resilience and ability to get more information out of the interviewee. I would ask more specific questions in response to their answers, so that I could get more information. Furthermore, I would want to see more interviews with other people as it would help me understand better techniques.  Next time I have an interview, I think I will take more notes around what they were saying, not just the actual script of the interview, as this would maybe explain in a deeper way the meaning behind hat the interview was trying to say.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
