2025-01-25 13:53:16,993 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': 600, 'files': None, 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': '\n\nHuman: Please grade the following student solution based on the exercise description and grading rubric provided.\n\nExercise Description:\nExecise instructions I write these lines to provide some advise and ease your minds to undertake the exam for the subject ‘Consumer Market Research’.  The exam is based on the cooperative Design Sprint exercise we undertook at class by teams. Specifically it will focus on the last stage which is the Customer Interview. For such you will have to execute one, two or more interviews, preferably the latter. In this case, the interviews can be done by different team members. The interviews must be either (remotely) simultaneously witnessed by all the other teams members. Or, the interview is recorded and shared. Once the interview is analyses the team should have a joint session to share their observations. The exam deliverable is a document written with any word processor with a minimum extensino of 1 page, not including front page if it exists. It is recommend including a front page with the project title and the name of the memebers in the team. It should answer the following topics: How did you prepare the environment for the interviews?  AHow did you prepare the environment for the team analysis of the observations? Which were you difficulties for finding interviews?  What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? Which were the observations that generated the mos discussion or took the longest time?\n\nGrading Rubric:\nHere are the rules and heuristics applied for grading the different categories. The criteria follow a consistent structure and syntax to ensure clarity and precision. <Category> Clarity </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is exceptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nMoana   Wetsuit   Interview       Name:   Flynn   Kuijsters     Group:   Aline,   Victor,   David,   Mehdi,   Luc        The   interview   was   conducted   with   a   student   living   in   Carcavelos,   Portugal   called   Robbe   van   Onsem.   He   surfs   about   twice   a   week   and   is   an   enviromentally   conscious   individual.   For   these   reasons,   he   fits   our   target   audience   based   on   his   interests   and   location.     How   did   you   prepare   for   the   environment   for   the   interview?     Due   to   the   interviewee’ s   location   being   elsewhere   than   Madrid,   the   interview   was   conducted   virtually   on   a   video   call.   In   order   to   make   the   interviewee   comfortable   and   not   overwhelmed,   only   myself   and   one   other   group   member   turned   their   camera   and   conducted   the   interview   whilst   the   others   were   spectators.   In   order   to   be   prepared   during   the   interview ,   I   made   sure   to   have   images   of   our   design   sprint   deck   and   images   of   our   wetsuit   product   ready   to   show   by   sharing   my   screen.   The   roles   of   each   group   member   were   also   discussed   by   having   clearly   defined   interviewers,   note-takers   and   an   interview-recorder .     How   did   you   prepare   for   the   team   analysis   of   the   observations?     As   a   team   we   analyzed   our   project   goals   and   objectives   and   how   these   could   be   aligned   throughout   the   interview .   Based   on   this   as   well   as   the   interview   guideline   provided,   we   made   predefined   questions   to   ask   during   the   interview .   By   doing   so,   we   could   spark   initial   thinking   from   the   interviewee   as   a   platform   for   observation   questions   that   would   arise   during   the   interview .   Thus   we   could   combine   our   current   understanding   of   our   project   and   the   understanding   and   observations   that   arose   from   the   interview .       What   were   the   difficulties   in   finding   interviewees?     The   main   difficulty   in   finding   interviewees   was   finding   an   individual   in   our   known   circles   that   was   either   an   industry   professional   or   part   of   our   target   audience.   It   was   important   for   us   to   interview   either   one   or   both   of   these   profiles   to   gain   valuable   insights   and   observations.   Since   the   target   audience   was   quite   specific,   we   ended   up   finding   one   candidate   for   the   interview .   Other   difficulties   that   we   encountered   were   finding   interviewees   we   could   interview   in   English   due   to   the   internatinality   of   the   group.   We   felt   that   translating   the   interview   would   take   away   from   the   quality   of   observation   quality .           What   would   you   consider   would   help   reaching   5   interviewees?     In   order   to   find   interviewees   belonging   to   the   target   audience   of   our   wetsuit   product,   next   time   it   would   be   good   to   contact   and   target   relevant   communities   or   organizations.   By   doing   so,   we   could   reach   5   interviewees   that   fit   the   profile   of   who   we   would   like   to   interview .   Furthermore,   next   time   we   could   also   find   and   contact   industry   professionals   on   LinkedIn   for   a   potential   interview .   This   could   be   individuals   involved   with   or   working   for   wetsuit   brands.   If   this   does   not   work,   we   could   also   widen   our   scope   to   certain   elements   of   the   target   audience   rather   than   trying   to   find   potential   interviewees   that   fit   all   the   characteristics   of   the   target   audience.       What   are   the   most   suppressive   comments   from   the   interview?     The   interviewee   described   that   he   currently   rents   the   gear   to   go   surfing   including   the   board   and   wetsuit.   For   this   reason,   he   mentioned   that   his   biggest   current   struggle   with   wetsuits   is   that   they   are   worn   by   several   others   and   therefore   have   holes   in   them   that   allow   water   to   get   in   and   therefore   loses   the   necessary   heat   to   stay   warm.   Furthermore,   the   wetsuits   gets   stretched   out   by   others   wearing   it   and   therefore   it’s   a   struggle   for   him   to   find   the   perfect   fit   for   him   while   surfing.    When   these   other   people   wear   the   wetsuits,   he   also   has   to   go   into   the   water   wearing   an   already   wet   suit   which   is   uncomfortable.   While   these   were   his   comments   that   described   the   most   negative   experiences,   he   mentioned   that   since   they   are   rentals,   there   aren’t   any   solutions   for   improvements   he   could   think   of   in   the   short   term.       Which   observations   generated   the   most   discussion   or   took   the   longest   time?     The   observation   that   generated   the   most   discussion   and   therefore   took   the   longest   was   whether   our   Moana   wetsuit   would   solve   his   current   issues   he   faces   whenever   he   goes   surfing.   He   was   concerned   that   due   to   the   additional   zips   in   the   wet   suit   that   allow   the   user   to   take   off   the   extended   arm   and   leg   sections,   water   would   get   into   the   suit   and   cause   him   to   get   cold   on   the   water .   This   was   a   large   concern   for   him   due   to   surfing   all-year   round   in   the   cold   Atlantic   Ocean   waters   on   the   Portugese   coast.   Furthermore,   he   is   not   planning   on   living   in   Carcavelos   in   the   long   term   and   therefore   was   hesitant   towards   buying   a   wetsuit.   He   discussed   that   for   while   he   is   there   for   his   studies,   it   is   much   more   financially   worthwhile   to   rent   wetsuits   as   opposed   to   buying   one.   This   makes   it   less   likely   for   him   to   purchase   the   Moana   wetsuit   which   would   likely   be   more   expensive   than   the   average   wetsuit   due   to   its   focus   on   sustainable   materials   and   production   as   well   as   the   additional   zip.   Finally ,   the   design   of   the   wetsuit   was   also   a   concern   for   him.   We   wanted   to   create   a   wetsuit   that   was   innovative   in   its   design   but   he   actually   preferred   the   traditional   look   of   wetsuits.   This   was   an   interesting   observation   for   us   that   also   generated   a   long   discussion.       INDIVIDUAL   SECTION   How   would   you   prepare   yourself   to   use   the   interview   techniques   in   your   projects,   initiatives   or   jobs   This   interview   was   very   good   practice   for   the   interview   techniques   and   guidelines   that   were   provided.   By   structuring   the   interview   with   a   friendly   welcome,   contextual   questions,   prototype   introduction,   tasks   and   nudges   and   a   brief   summary ,   we   were   able   to   effectively   attain   information   and   observations   from   the   interviewee.   Therefore   for   future   interviews   for   projects,   initiatives   or   jobs,   whether   I’m   the   interviewer   or   interviewee,   I   will   write   up   this   structure   to   visualize   and   plan   the   interview .   Furthermore,   based   on   the   techniques   provided   and   what   I   have   learned   from   this   interview   process,   I   would   also   incorporate   the   following   elements   into   future   interviews.   These   are   understanding   the   purpose   and   context,   developing   a   structured   interview   plan,   research   and   anticipate   challenges   that   may   arise,   build   rapport   with   interviewees   and   analyze   and   use   feedback.   Finally ,   this   interviewee   also   showed   me   the   importance   of   the   questions   that   arise   from   discussion   during   the   interview .   Although   the   pre-defined   questions   are   important   towards   achieving   the   goals   and   objectives   of   the   interview ,   it   is   vital   to   dig   deeper   into   understanding   what   the   interviewee   is   discussing   in   their   answers.   Therefore   in   future   interviews,   I   would   definitely   attempt   to   create   a   conversation   with   the   interviewee   through   the   questions   and   answers   that   arise.       What   would   you   consider   improving   for   obtaining   more   learning   from   the   interviews?   I   would   consider   conducting   more   interviews   so   that   there   are   several   different   interviewers.   Due   to   the   fact   that   we   only   conducted   one   interview ,   there   were   only   two   actual   interviewers.   During   our   interview ,   I   noticed   that   the   other   interviewer   thought   much   differently   and   asked   different   kinds   of   follow-up   questions   than   I   did.   For   this   reason,   if   we   conducted   additional   interviews,   different   ideas   and   questions   could   arise   due   to   different   people   being   the   interviewers.   Furthermore,   next   time   I   would   have   personalized   our   pre-defined   questions   as   well   as   made   them   more   open-ended.   By   doing   so,   the   interviewee   is   required   to   think   more   about   their   answer   and   provide   a   more   detailed   answer   to   the   questions   therefore   allowing   for   more   potential   of   new   observations   and   discussions.   Having   a   wide   range   of   observations   and   opinions   is   crucial   for   a   project   with   a   product   like   ours.   Therefore   having   a   diverse   range   of   individuals   within   our   target   audience   based   on   nationality ,   location,   gender ,   age   and   interests   would   provide   new   and   interesting   observations,   answers,   discussions   an   ideas.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
