ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nConsumer Market Research Interview Summary Essay Agentify Sports Agency Group Members: Loukemy Billard, Sai-Rahul Jajimogga, Margaux Veyne, Julia Castro Lobo, Isaias Gutierrez Gil, Alejandra Carmargo and Go-Molemo Moloabi Author/Writer: Go-Molemo Moloabi Interviews regarding the assessment of the Agentify platform obtained valuable insights from our expert interview/consultation with Angel Guardiola, ESBS Sports Events Lecturer/Professor, who has a number of years of experience in the sports sponsorship and brand collaboration business. This analysis will cover four main topics of discussion: how the interview environment was prepared and the methodology regarding the analysis of the team, complications related to finding qualified interviewees, the feedback with the highest perceived value, and the observations which created the most team discussions. This structured approach allowed us to identify key areas that needed improvement while also confirming some aspects of our design and functionality of the platform. The interview with Angel Guardiola was structured around the interview guidelines provided, which ensured consistency and focus in conducting the session. In using Bubble.io as a platform for development, the form of questioning was influenced, especially when focusing on user interface and functionality aspects. The observation matrix - Agentify Interview Sheet.xlsx. Which allowed us to systematically record responses across key areas of interest, such as brand identity, user experience, and technical implementation. The following detailed examination presents our findings and recommendations based on this expert interview, which has proved instrumental in shaping the future development of the Agentify platform.  The setting of the interview was prepared on Microsoft Teams, as the base platform for an ideal environment concerning remote communication and recording. Before conducting the interview with Angel Guardiola, the team agreed upon the plan for documentation and prepared structured observation templates with the purpose of collecting both verbal feedback and non-verbal signals. A joint session was conducted during the team analysis phase where members shared the observations systematically taken during the main areas of focus: brand identity, user experience, and strategic areas. This helped in systematically organizing all the information gathered from the expert interview regarding sponsorship processes and functionality of the platform for more effective analysis. The biggest challenge in securing interviews was identifying experts who could comprehensively provide insight into sponsorship and brand collaboration in sports. As much as we were able to interview Angel Guardiola, finding other qualified interviewees was rather challenging due to their limited availability, shortage of time, and the specialized nature of our platform\'s focus. While explaining and trying to reach the target of five interviewees, we would recommend increasing our outreach by: using our network with brand partners such as El Corte Inglés; reaching out to sports federations; event management professionals; using professional networks in the sports industry. This, together with flexibility regarding scheduling and added value propositions for the participation of experts, would secure more expert interviews. The most effective feedback from the interview with Angel Guardiola was related to a number of critical aspects that need serious attention on the platform. First, he identified the profile section as highly problematic, requiring too much information upfront, and it should be completely redesigned into multiple stages. Another striking observation was the lack of diversity in the visual content on the platform; this needs immediate attention. He further elaborated that the missing key elements include the call-to-action buttons and direct access to the blogs from within the newsletter section, which impede the degree of user interaction. This comment was suppressive because such a comment pointed toward fundamental flaws in the design of the platform, which could hurt its core use and adoption. Extensive discussion and analysis time was made based on three critical areas of the site. The registration process involved a lot of discussion, especially about the amount of information required and how it should be reorganized into steps. The newsletter function was discussed at great length by the team, who debated its shortcomings, such as the lack of direct access to the blog, the inability to attach news articles, and the frequent updating of content. Considerable discussion time was spent on the content strategy of the platform, especially on how variety could be included in the form of sponsorship opportunities, competition coverage, and results tracking. Specifically, the team was engrossed in how such elements would be integrated into the platform while keeping the interface clean and intuitive. How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives or jobs? And, what do you consider Improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews? To arm yourself for using interview techniques in your projects, initiatives, or jobs, a broad approach is necessary. Do some deep readings of both the subject matter and the persons you will interview. Get familiarized with structured, semi-structured, and unstructured approaches to interviewing to pick out which one will work best for you. Practice active listening skills and construct open-ended questions that foster lengthy responses. Further, make an interview guide of topics that will be covered and some possible probes for complete coverage. In addition, the skill of establishing rapport in a short time will help in getting good information. You may also conduct mock interviews with colleagues or friends to better your skills and instill confidence. With interviewing, this usually means the need for improvement in analytics and systematic elaboration of results for further data processing. Right after each interview, try to sum up in your head what has been heard and what meanings or patterns seem to emerge across conversations. Develop an excellent note-taking method or even tape-record interviews if a respondent does not object to preserve minute details. Improve your interviewing skills over time by reflecting on the interviews you have conducted, soliciting feedback from interviewees or your peers where appropriate. Keep informed about new interview methods and tools that may be helpful in collecting and analyzing data. In this way, you will be able to continually improve your skills and be more effective at gathering valuable information from interviews and applying it appropriately to your projects or initiatives. Documentation and Additional Research/Information Our analysis was supplemented by: Detailed observation matrices documenting user interactions Technical documentation from Bubble.io implementation Comparative analysis of similar platforms in the sports sponsorship space Integration of Design Sprint principles in the evaluation process This comprehensive approach, combining technical development insights with user experience research, has provided a robust foundation for future platform improvements and development directions. Roles and Responsibilities Makers: Loukemy Billard and Go-Molemo Moloabi Stitchers: Margaux Veyne and Alejandra Carmargo Asset Collector: Collector: Sai-Rahul Jajimogga Writer: Julia Castro Lobo Interviewer: Isaias Gutierrez Gil Makers: Bubble Apps: https://agentify.bubbleapps.io/version-test/ai_landing_page_1  Stitchers and Asset Collector Work https://www.canva.com/design/DAGccnfDe7c/4kioFg35l7gmz3a9tvxTBQ/edit?utm_content=DAGccnfDe7c&utm_campaign=designshare&utm_medium=link2&utm_source=sharebutton  Interviewer Work: Agentify Prototype Interview-20250107_160339-Grabación de la  reunión.mp4  Writers Work:\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
