ews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nHow did you prepare the environment for the interviews   Technical preparation:   Extensive testing of the prototype of Agentify across various devices for make sure our  website was responsive  including desktop, mobile and tablet computers.   We did a meeting with the prototype teams for preparing with the potential technical  challenges.   Prepared  an interview script:    The tasks included the review of the homepage, carrying out the sign -up process as well  as navigating through the newsletter section.   For the Interview we had a n interviewer Isaia who moderated the session to allow for a  natural style of interaction.   Post Interview Debriefing: The debriefing was held to commence as soon as the  interview ended     Challenges Faced:   Niche Market: The sports sponsorship and networking industry is quite specialized,  which narrows down the number of potential participants.    Leveraging Networks: We reached out to our personal and professional contacts within  the sports sector, and we decided to interview  Angel Guardiola sport event Manager  who is also our Sport event teacher     What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?     Was her difficulty to find some features inside our website     Inside join us brand he didn’t understand he found an empty page with only an image,   and  he didn’t understand that to get  more information he needed to click to an icon with  an arrow .   He didn’t click on the logo    And about the newsletter section he said that he didn’t know how much he has  to scroll   He noted the absence of a button linking the newsletter section directly to the blog and  recommended using this space for sharing news articles with a strong call -to-action  (CTA). This unexpected suggestion opened a new way to increase user engagement.   Clearly outlining its purpose and including updated content (e.g., sponsorship,  competitions, and results).              What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?     During the interview , Ángel gave great feedback on this project.   The most suppressive comments were  around the sign -up Process in her globality and   her design aspect     He says that the log-in and the  sign -up page was intuitively  functional.  During the  conception of one website making  your website functional  is the hardest aspect because  you must  include different  workflow add some  link for storage the data information  etc...    He said  also that it was interesting to have created  a button for login as an athlete and  another one for login as a brand.     The most important problem was around the editing profile page just after the login  page     in her opinion we included too much information  with too many invasive questions.   Reorganizing the profile section into 2–3 steps , starting with only the  mandatory questions.   Removing redundant fields, e.g., “brand” or “athlete,” which are already  specified earlier in the process.   Including targeted follow -up fields, e.g., if the user is an athlete, ask for their  type (e.g., trainer, coach, or active athlete).   Delaying invasive questions like biography  to a later stage.     This feedback was interesting because I realized finally, he didn’t complete the  profile section during the interview  because it would have taken too many long.  The problem was because of it he didn’t have access  to the profile ( the  most   important part of our website. It was  quite a shame  that we didn’t get any  feedback on this section.       Here  is an example of one profile page create with all our features           Opinion about the interview how can we make the interview more  relevant?      I found all her feedback was pertinent but i think for any website project there is  a big focus only about the design aspect and unfortunately nor about the  functionality it could have been good to interview also a website conceptor for  get more knowledge and feedback on the technical aspects.   Our question was also not enough oriented around this aspect               This challenge made me realize the importance of combining  user feedback, common  ideas, and technology tools to create new and relevant solutions. I gained experience  using Bubble, learned about data management, plugins, workflow automation, and  developed features to create workflow models.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
