ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nEXAM Consumer Market Research Interview for Agentify’s web prototype with Angel Guardiola To prepare for the interview, we reached out to our \\"Sports Events\\" professor, Ángel Guardiola, who has extensive expertise in dealing with sponsorships and companies for his events. We believed his experience would provide valuable insights for our project. After the holidays, we asked him for an interview, and he kindly accepted. On the day of the interview, I, as the interviewer, was the only one with the camera on, while the rest of the team attended with their cameras off to not overwhelm our guest. We started the conversation informally, discussing the holidays, before transitioning into the main questions of the interview. For the team analysis of the observations, we collected notes taken by each team member during the interview. To streamline the process, we decided to focus on the most significant points—those that everyone had noted as important. This collaborative approach ensured that we prioritized the most relevant insights from the session. After contacting Ángel Guardiola for our first interview, we sought additional leads by asking our academic coordinators, Paloma and Carmen, if they knew someone involved in sponsorships or agencies. They suggested Professor Alessandro Carlini, who works in a sports agency and will be our negotiation professor next semester. Unfortunately, despite reaching out to him, we did not receive a response. The biggest difficulty in finding interviews was the timing, as the winter holidays created significant scheduling challenges. Even coordinating the interview with Ángel had to wait until after Día de Reyes Magos. Another limitation was our limited network of contacts within the fields of sponsorships and agencies, which narrowed our options. In the future, having more time to schedule interviews outside the holiday season would greatly help. Additionally, having access to someone already involved in the industry, like Professor Alessandro, could make a big difference. He could have easily referred us to potential interviewees and expanded our network. Ángel Guardiola provided valuable feedback on the \\"Agentify\\" prototype. He appreciated the project name, describing it as clear and effective in reflecting the purpose of the initiative. However, there were also some suppressive comments. He identified some areas for improvement in the design and presentation. For instance, the logo appeared unpolished on the homepage and required refinement. Additionally, he suggested including more inclusive photos to better represent a diverse audience and enhance the project\'s appeal. To improve user navigation, Ángel recommended reorganizing the menu structure, starting with a clear definition of the project’s purpose, followed by sections tailored to athletes and brands, and ending with the \\"Contact Us\\" section. Ángel also provided specific suggestions to improve the sign-up process and newsletter section. He found the sign-up process overly demanding, as it required too much information from users in a single stage. To address this, he proposed splitting the process into two stages, making it more user-friendly and encouraging completion. For the newsletter section, Ángel pointed out the absence of a button linking directly to the blog. He recommended utilizing this section to share news articles and incorporating a strong call-to-action (CTA) to engage users and guide them toward further interactions. In summary, Ángel identified three overarching areas for improvement in the project. First, he emphasized the importance of achieving design cohesion, ensuring all visual elements align seamlessly to create a professional and polished look. Second, he recommended adding warmth to the contact section, making it more inviting and user-friendly. Lastly, he highlighted the need to include real-life success stories or case studies, as these can build trust and provide concrete examples of the project’s value. During the team analysis, there wasn’t much debate since we generally agreed on Ángel’s comments. However, the aspects that took the most time during the interview itself revolved around achieving design cohesion and ensuring a uniform look across the page. One issue that stood out early on was the logo, which had one letter from \\"Agentify\\" missing outside the homepage. This detail highlighted the importance of consistency in design and sparked a focused discussion on improving visual elements to enhance the overall professional appeal of the project. Finally, to effectively use the interview technique from a design sprint in my projects or jobs, I would ensure thorough preparation by defining the interview’s purpose and tailoring the questions to extract actionable insights, as we did with Ángel Guardiola. Learning from our challenges, I would improve the structure by ensuring that every team member actively contributes, whether through note-taking or live questions, and aligning on priorities beforehand. I also think a live interview is way better than online because it allows you to bond deeper with the interviewee. In general, I think this technique is useful to extract valuable firsthand insights directly from professionals in their field of work, but it is important to have the right connections. In conclusion, I think the design sprint is a powerful tool for identifying project flaws and fostering innovation. It addresses issues early, ensuring user-centered and practical solutions. For me this approach is invaluable for future work, as it encourages creative problem-solving while staying aligned with project goals, making it ideal for driving efficient and impactful innovation. Resources: Link to Interview Matrix: https://aceeducation998-my.sharepoint.com/:x:/g/personal/margaux_veyne_alumni_ace-education_es/EWLnhFj36W9NsuyO4rmMswABVKl8MHO92Ui79XiHgpR3pg?e=Fd78Kn&nav=MTVfe0UyRUQ1MTg5LUI3NkQtNDUxMi1CN0NCLTA5OTRDRTkwRjFCM30  Link to Web page prototype: https://agentify.bubbleapps.io/version-test/ai_landing_page_1?debug_mode=true  Link to Interview with Angel (ACE education account necessary): Agentify Prototype Interview-20250107_160339-Grabación de la reunión.mp4 Interview Script (ACE education account necessary): CONSUMER MARKET RESEARCH interview.docx\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
