ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nBautista Palma   Consumer Market Research   GSBM – Group A    Design Sprint Project   Level Up     GSBM   Group A   Submission Date:   17/01/2025     Professor   Juan Vazquez     Team Members   Bautista Palma   Anastiia Lemishko   Soledad Ansaldo   Paula Carrasco   Anaïs Venkatasawmy             Bautista Palma   Consumer Market Research   GSBM – Group A    Interview Report   • How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? And, the same for the team  analysis of the observations?   Before scheduling a date, time and type of interview, our team gathered to set the plan and  framework, following the interview guidelines shared with us and the recommended  questions and steps.   The interview took place on the 7th of January of 2025 at 2pm on Zoom´s platform (online).   It was conducted by Anaïs, who was the one that asked the questions and talked with the  interviewee.  Meanwhile the rest of us were watching the interview on the platform.  Because of the holiday peri od it was hard to set up a place to conduct the interview, that is  why we decided to have it online, so as to also make it easier for the interviewee.   To begin with, the interviewer kindly welcomed the interviewee, thanking her for agreeing to  participate and asking about how were her holidays. This led to the introduction of the  interviewee, who presented herself and her actual situation. After understa nding and  having a clear idea of her concerns, Anaïs presented her the purpose of our project and how  we think it will be very helpful for her. Then, the prototype of our website was introduced,  which consisted on the first steps of how a person in her sit uation would find it, sign up and  be able to access some main features.   While this took place, the rest of the team was very attentive to any details, reactions,  suggestions and comments the interviewee made during each step. After the presentation  of the prototype, Anaïs conducted some final questions to summarize the idea an d the  feedback of the interviewee.   After the interview finished, our team met online so as to put together our observations and  personal opinions. In the following table we can see the observational matrix we created to  analyze the most important insights from the interview.         • Which were you difficulties for finding interviews? What would you consider would  help to reach 5 interviewees?   The most complicated issue for us was finding someone who would agree to help us in  just a few weeks’ notice, especially considering that we had the end of the years holidays   Bautista Palma   Consumer Market Research   GSBM – Group A    in between. Also, it was easier for us to find a player profile than an agent one. We do not  have the network and contacts for finding a potential or current female football agent in  such short time.  This would have been very useful for us, because in tha t case we would  be able to have not only a perspective and feedback from the players side, but also from  an agent opinion and view.   If we had had more time, I think we would be able to get 5 interviews. Especially with the  many social media platforms and job posting options that are available. Also, we could  have met with the volunteering and job opportunity coordinator from ESBS to he lp us  know who to contact, for example via LinkedIn.     • What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?   The most suppressive comment and observation we got from the interview was that our  signing up section took too long to be completed, and that this made the process not  user friendly and easy to access as we wanted since we started the project. When we  discussed this issue after the interview, we realized that we need to work on the  prioritization of the boxes that need to be completed during the signing up window.   Also, when he showed the interviewee the “Track” button that appeared on her profile, it  was not as clear as we thought that it takes you to another section where you can  visualize your updated stats from your matches and performances. We considered  changing the text of the button to “Stats” or “Statistics”.     • Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest  time?   As it was mentioned before, the observation that took more time to discuss was the one  of the signing up process. It is a very relevant phase of the process (because this is the  information the agents would be able to access when they are looking for playe rs to  contact), and we really have to think this through and define if it is better for this section  to be more user friendly but with less information, or the other way around. The ideal  solution would be to create a signing up process that it is easy for  the player to complete,  and that it gathers the most relevant information an agent would like to see on a potential  player profile.     • How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects,  initiatives or jobs. And, what you consider improving for obtaining more learning  from the interviews.   I believe the interview and Sprint Design model are very useful, specially for new personal  projects. Nowadays, I have some ideas for starting my new business, and these models  are a very easy, accessible and cheap way of obtaining really relevant informat ion about  what I want to offer, how to improve it and understand what are the features that call the  attention of potential clients the most.   If I do start my own business, I would definitely use this practical tools, but prepare  myself much better and with a different time schedule. Specially on the selection of the  Bautista Palma   Consumer Market Research   GSBM – Group A    interviewees. If you do not interview the correct person or prospect profile you are aiming  to become your client, all the feedback and information that you will gather from the  process won’t be very useful.   So, in that case, I would focus mostly on creating an accurate potential prospect idea.  This way, I would have a clear understanding of which profiles should I look for to  interview.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
