ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nDesign Sprint Customer Interview          Consumer Market Research Exam Luc Bausch      ESBS 2025  Table of Contents 1. Interview and Analysis Preparation .......................................................................................... 3 How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? ..................................................................... 3 How did you prepare for the team analysis of the observations?............................................................. 3 2. Finding Interviewees ................................................................................................................ 3 Which were your difficulties for finding interviews? ................................................................................ 3 What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? .............................................................. 3 3. Comments from the interview.................................................................................................. 4 What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? .............................................................. 4 4. Observations and Discussions................................................................................................... 4 Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time? .................. 4 5. Exam Question ......................................................................................................................... 4 How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives, or jobs? .... 4 What do you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews? ................................ 5       1. Interview and Analysis Preparation How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? We prepared our questions in advance to ensure a focused and structured conversation. Only two members of our group attended the interview to avoid overwhelming the interviewee and to keep the setting comfortable. The interview was conducted via a video call (teams), which was recorded to ensure accuracy and provide the entire team with access to the session for later analysis. A transcript of the interview was also prepared to facilitate detailed examination. Furthermore, we decided to ask different questions from different categories, starting with broader questions/welcome questions, then afterwards contextual questions and last but not least we concluded with specific questions about our wetsuit. How did you prepare for the team analysis of the observations? The recorded video and transcript were shared with all team members, enabling everyone to independently analyze the interview session. Then a collaborative team session was conducted, to get more structure between the interview answers and the task questions. This ensured that all insights were collectively reviewed and discussed. 2. Finding Interviewees Which were your difficulties for finding interviews? We faced no difficulties in finding this interviewee, as they were a friend of one of our group members. The participant was an amateur surfer who practices regularly and is active in various sports. This connection made the process straightforward and seamless. Definitely, it would be more difficult, if we had to find someone to interview, who we didn’t know at the start. Knowing someone, who is into surfing and could participate in our interview, made it easier for us to go through the whole interview process. To conclude, our strategy to find an interviewee, was “asking people we know”. What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? If we had to find five interviewees, our strategy would involve starting with friends or acquaintances within the surfing community and asking them to participate. Additionally, we would ask our initial interviewee to connect us with others in the surfing industry who might be willing to help. That could lead us to many people from this specific industry and so also many potential interviewees. If we wanted more and further diversification of our interviewees,  we would leverage social media platforms to reach potential and passionate participants and broaden our interview pool. 3. Comments from the interview What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? The participant emphasized the challenges of renting wetsuits, particularly issues with the ones already used and wet before, improper fit, water getting into the wetsuit, and difficulty in putting them on when wet. They also highlighted the importance of having or finding a wetsuit that is perfectly sealed to prevent water from entering, which significantly affects their surfing experience. Right now their biggest struggle is to find those wetsuits perfectly sealed and not allowing water to get in. Finding the perfect wetsuit is hard, that is why it will be hard too for us to create it and ensure everything is perfect. 4. Observations and Discussions Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time? The most debated observation was the importance of ensuring the wetsuit is waterproof, particularly at the zippers. The participant’s concern about water seeping through detachable parts led to extended discussions on the design’s functionality and the need to maintain the core purpose of a wetsuit. The interviewee also mentioned, that his ideal experience would be a dry and warm wetsuit, that has the perfect size, no water gets into it and stays warm during surfing. Therefore, the interviewee wanted to be sure, that our wetsuit would provide all the necessary functions from a good wetsuit. 5. Exam Question How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives, or jobs? To effectively use the interview technique in future projects, I would start by clearly defining the goals and objectives of the interview and preparing a comprehensive set of questions tailored to the subject. Assigning specific roles to team members (e.g., interviewer, note-taker, observer) and conducting technical checks would ensure a smooth process. Using structured tools like the observation matrix and ensuring access to recordings and transcripts would enhance the accuracy of data collection and analysis. During the interview, I would make sure, that there is a good working environment and that everyone feels comfortable, both the interviewee and the interviewers (myself included).   What do you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews? To improve learning outcomes, I would focus on conducting practice runs to refine the interview process and questions. Also, encouraging participants to provide open-ended responses and observing both verbal and non-verbal cues/signals would help gather deeper insights. Additionally, including frequent team debriefs to discuss findings step-by-step would avoid information overload and foster collaborative understanding. Last but not least, I would include feedback in the interview, so that future interviews can be adapted and improved.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
