ptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nAgentify  Margaux Veyne,  Rahul, Alejandra Camargo,   Gomolemo, Isaias, Julia Castrolobo and Loukemy       Consumer Market Research Exam – Interview Report    How did you prepare the environment for the interviews?  In preparation for our interview, we meticulously followed the interview guidelines. The interview took  place on the 7th of January of 2025 at 16h00 on Microsoft teams (online).  It was conducted by Isaias,  who was the one that asked the questions and talked with the interviewee (Angel Guardiola).  Meanwhile the rest of us were watching the interview on the platform with our cameras turned off in  order to not overwhelm Angel.   The interview, started with a friendly welcome to our interviewee, including New Year\'s greetings and  asking him how his vacation had been. After establishing a comfortable and more relaxed setting,  Isaias formally introduced the interview\'s purpose, explicitly noting the recording and outlining a  structured approach: first exploring the interviewee\'s sponsorship experiences, then presenting our  prototype, and ultimately requesting honest feedback.   The initial phase focused on contextual questioning, strategically designed to gain insights into the  interviewee\'s professional background in the sports sponsorship industry. This was done in order to  set a robust foundation for a comprehensive and meaningful discussion about our prototype and its  purpose.     How did you prepare the environment for the team analysis of the observations?  During the interview we filled in notes and the design sprint observation matrix. We then conducted a  meeting where we compared all of our notes and created a list of reoccurring issues and themes. We  also discussed our priority rankings for each feedback pieces.    Which were you difficulties for finding interviewees? What would you consider would help to  reach 5 interviewees?  The main challenges we faced to find interviewees was the timing, finding availabilities during end of  the year festivities was very complicated. In addition finding experts in the field of sponsorships,  agents and the sports industry was equally a struggle. A possible solution to find 5 interviewees would  be to post on LinkedIn a post about searching for experts in the field of sponsorships in the sports  industry for a mockup presentation.     What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?  The most suppressive comments from our interviewee were taken from our Design Sprint Observation  Matrix (Appendix 1)  and consolidated in a summary of themes:   1. Logo issues: Bad size, definition, and quality. Missing an \\"i\\" in \\"Agentify”.  2. Navigation concerns:  a. Language tab missing taskbar  b. Bad placement of AI Bot in taskbar   c. Newsletter section difficult to find, should be in the taskbar  d. Inconsistent signup process for brands and athletes  3. Content and layout:  a. Lack of diversity and inclusivity in homepage visuals  b. Missing clear objective or aim of the project on the homepage  c. Font too small in some sections  d. Spelling mistakes in a few areas  4. User experience:  a. Inconsistent presentation of \\"Why join us\\" sections for brands and athletes  b. Missing call-to-action buttons for quick signup  Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time?   The top 3 observations that generated the most discussion were:   1. Logo mistake in spelling and quality (Appendix 2)   - Should be “Agentify” not “Agentfy”  - Reoccurring issue and shows a very careless and  unprofessional perspective of our company  2. Taskbar in homepage of website (Appendix 3)   - The components included in the taskbar were not in a correct order   - Some components missing from the task bar making it hard to navigate the page  - The task bar should include in this order:   1. About us  2. Join us athlete   3. Join us brand   4. Newsletter   5. Log in   6. Contact us   7. AI Bot   3. Lack of cohesion and efficiency in section to sell product (Appendix 4)   - No coherence in theme and layout in the “join us brand” and “join us athlete” sections  - One section has automatic information while the other requires clicking a button for the  information to pop up.  - No call-to-action buttons in either sections which is not efficient as users have to go back  to homepage to join. Too many steps.     How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives or  jobs?   To prepare myself for using the interview technique from the design sprint day 5 in my projects,  initiatives, or jobs I would incorporate some of the instructions in my future life.   When handling any future project, initiative or jobs I will always ensure to have a clear objective and  structure which are aligned with my project goals. I will also follow the idea of the design sprint layout  where I will ensure each member has a specific role and is assigned specific tasks to maximize  efficiency.   When it comes to participating in interviews where I am the interviewee I will inspire myself by the in  design sprint interview layout since it allows for optimal contact and maximization of information  extraction.  1. Friendly welcome (5 minutes)  2. Contextual questions (10 minutes)  3. Interview itself (15min)  4. Brief summary (5 minutes)  In addition from this interview technique it came to my attention that skills can be developed from this  interviewing technique. These skills include:   - Practicing active listening techniques to capture exact quotes and observations.   - Learning to maintain a neutral stance and avoid leading questions.   - Develop non-verbal cue and body language observation skills.  These skills would help me not only in a professional setting but also in daily life as these are  important in any communication setting.  Lastly, when filling out the observation matrix I enjoyed the idea of color-coding for different types of  reactions (e.g., green for positive, light red for negative). This will be something I will use in project  planning and other tasks in order to update progress (green for done, yellow for in progress and red  for not done/late)     What do you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews?   In order to improve the results from the interview itself using more elaborate interview techniques  would give more information. Utilizing the \\"5 Whys\\" technique would allow to dig deeper into  participant responses and uncover root causes of behaviors or preferences. In addition implementing  the \\"Critical Incident Technique (CIT)\\" to gather specific examples of positive and negative  experiences related to the product or service being discussed. CIT is a flexible method that typically  encompasses five major areas: determining and reviewing the incident, fact-finding through collecting  details from participants, identifying issues, deciding on resolutions based on various possible  solutions, and finally, evaluating to ensure the selected solution addresses the root cause without  causing further problems. In addition asking for feedback with the sandwich method as usually having  free feedback sessions results mostly in negatives without highlighting the positives. The sandwich  method is a feedback layout where constructive criticism or negative feedback is \\"sandwiched\\"  between two positive statements.   In addition to develop further in the data collection and analysis, adding a section to record emotional  reactions (using emojis for quick visual reference) in the matrix would facilitate recording non-verbal  cues. Adding a task assessment form with task completion rates, time to complete tasks and number  of clicks or steps taken to complete the task would further the analysis of asking for the interviewee to  conduct tasks and would give numerical data to assess the mockup itself.     Appendix:   Appendix 1. Design Sprint Observation Matrix  Time User Action/Quote Comments Priority   16:00 Start of interview    16:25 User starts navigating on webpage Okay look and feel    16:25 User looks at logo and task bar Name \\"Agentify\\" is clear of what the purpose of the  brand is. Good naming.     Bad logo: bad size and definition and quality H    Would expect to see a language tab  M    Unsure of what the AI Bot is first M    Add a newsletter section in menu  M  16:27 User looks at home page Colours: \\"grey blue colours = seriousness and  technical feel\\" correct colour pallet     Involve more diversity and more inclusion in home  page: \\"gender, race, physical ability\\" M    Slogan: \\"okay but might be overpromising\\" M    Add the objective or aim of the project somewhere  instead of contact us first M  16:33 User completes Signup Process Easily and quickly fills in all information to sign up      Pretty clear and intuitive     Not sure if you need phone number \\"too many  fields needed to be filled out\\"  M    Date of Birth is not needed M    Why is there an option to sign up as an Athlete or a  brand, should be two different signup sheets M    Asking too much when simply signing up to be a  member   The creation of the profile a should be done later  when the user wants to H  16:38 User looks for section \'why join us\'  as a Brand Struggles to find the information about why the  Brand should join us  H    Nice explanation as to why Agentify can help the  brand      No call to action; a quick button to be a member  right under is necessary  H  16:45 User looks for section \'why join us\'  as a Athlete Logo is missing an “I” \\"Agentfy\\" on logo H    Not coherent, “join as a brand” you need to click for  a pop up while in “join as an athlete” you have it  already displayed  H    Clearer for athlete sign up page      Font is too small  M    Again, missing call to action and need for a quick  button to sign up  H  16:48 Looks into another signup page Why does it say fans? And not brands H  16:49 Navigates to contact support Why is there no separate sections for contact us as  an athlete and as a brand M    The word “mumber” is incorrect should be  “member”   16:51 User looks for newsletter section Struggles to find the newletter section H    Needs to be included as a section in the task bar to  find more easily  H  16:54 User goes down to Sports  Connection area Likes to idea but needs to be clearer labelled and a  nice added value  M  16:56 general feedback could be something to look in a year but now it  needs a lot of work H    Appendix 2. Agentify Logo     Appendix 3. Agentify website taskbar     Appendix 4. “join us brand” and “join us athlete” sections\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
