2025-01-25 13:53:20,335 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': 600, 'files': None, 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': '\n\nHuman: Please grade the following student solution based on the exercise description and grading rubric provided.\n\nExercise Description:\nExecise instructions I write these lines to provide some advise and ease your minds to undertake the exam for the subject ‘Consumer Market Research’.  The exam is based on the cooperative Design Sprint exercise we undertook at class by teams. Specifically it will focus on the last stage which is the Customer Interview. For such you will have to execute one, two or more interviews, preferably the latter. In this case, the interviews can be done by different team members. The interviews must be either (remotely) simultaneously witnessed by all the other teams members. Or, the interview is recorded and shared. Once the interview is analyses the team should have a joint session to share their observations. The exam deliverable is a document written with any word processor with a minimum extensino of 1 page, not including front page if it exists. It is recommend including a front page with the project title and the name of the memebers in the team. It should answer the following topics: How did you prepare the environment for the interviews?  AHow did you prepare the environment for the team analysis of the observations? Which were you difficulties for finding interviews?  What would you consider would help to reach 5 interviewees? What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? Which were the observations that generated the mos discussion or took the longest time?\n\nGrading Rubric:\nHere are the rules and heuristics applied for grading the different categories. The criteria follow a consistent structure and syntax to ensure clarity and precision. <Category> Clarity </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is exceptionally clear and easily understood by the intended audience. All ideas are precise, concise, and free from ambiguity. 2. **8-9:** The writing is mostly clear, with minor instances of vagueness or complexity that do not hinder understanding significantly. 3. **6-7:** The writing is generally understandable but includes vague or unclear phrasing that could confuse the audience. 4. **4-5:** The writing is unclear or overly complex, requiring substantial effort from the reader to understand. 5. **1-3:** The writing is highly confusing, disorganized, or filled with ambiguous language. 6. **0:** The writing is unintelligible or entirely fails to communicate any ideas. </Heuristics> <Category> Coherence </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The essay flows seamlessly, with each idea logically connected to the next and the conclusion strongly tied to the preceding argument. 2. **8-9:** The essay is well-organized, with minor issues in transitions or connections between ideas. 3. **6-7:** The essay is moderately coherent but includes gaps where the logical flow is unclear or weak. 4. **4-5:** The essay is disorganized, with disconnected ideas or poor transitions that make following the argument difficult. 5. **1-3:** The essay lacks coherence entirely, with ideas presented in a haphazard or unrelated manner. 6. **0:** No logical structure or flow is present. </Heuristics> <Category> Grammar </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The writing is grammatically flawless, with no errors in syntax, punctuation, or spelling. 2. **8-9:** The writing has a few minor grammatical issues that do not impede understanding. 3. **6-7:** The writing has noticeable grammatical errors that slightly affect readability. 4. **4-5:** The writing has frequent grammatical mistakes that hinder comprehension. 5. **1-3:** The writing has pervasive grammatical issues, making it difficult to read. 6. **0:** The grammar is so poor that the writing is unintelligible. </Heuristics> <Category> Use of Interviews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nConsumer Market Research – Interview insights – Level Up Team members: Anaïs Venkatasawmy, Bautista Palma, Anastasiia Lemishko, Sole Ansaldo, Paula Carrasco How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? How did you prepare the environment for the team analysis of the observations? This interview took place online at home via Zoom. All members of the group were in attendance but only I, Anaïs, conducted the interview. To prepare for this interview, we had already decided on the questions to be asked and had decided to complete the design matrix together on a shared document during the call. We chose an online platform because many people were busy during the holiday period and this would be easier to schedule than an in-person interview. Once the interview started, I welcomed the interviewee and before getting on to the meat of the interview, I double checked that they were alright with us all being here. They agreed and reiterated that they were willing to give their insights as long as we did not use their name. Once these formalities were over, I explained the structure of the interview and what exactly they were to expect: first a brief introduction about their current situation, then a presentation using our design sprint sketches and lastly questions to summarise their overall thoughts regarding what we presented. Following the completion of the interview, we started a new meeting with the whole group, without the interviewee of course, to discuss our observations and finalise our design matrix. What were the difficulties you faced when looking for interviewees?  What do you think would help when trying to reach 5 interviewees? Unfortunately, looking for interviewees during such a busy holiday period was our main challenge. Most people we had reached out to had already planned out their upcoming few weeks and had no availability for a call with us. Furthermore, finding an amateur player was quite difficult and we had to search within our pre-existing networks to find someone who would fit our target audience due to the time constraints. To reach 5 interviewees, we would have had to start looking much more in advance. It would have been preferable to look further than just the people we are in touch with, so a potential posting online via a platform such as LinkedIn would have been conducive to this. What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? One of the main takeaways from our interview was that we should consider paid Google ads so that we will appear high up in Google Searches once we do make a functioning prototype. Secondly, we should try to streamline our sign-up process because it is too long and this may deter potential clients. Lastly, our “Track” button generated some confusion. For this, we could try to use different vocabulary to better convey what each section of our page would mean. Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time? During our post-interview team analysis, the observation that triggered the most discussion was the one regarding the sign-up process. We were trying to find a way to optimise it so that any potential clients stay engaged and aren’t put off by the amount of time it may take. This section is of utmost importance to us as this is how the players register with our site so this part would have to be worked on. How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects,  initiatives or jobs? I believe this interview process was very helpful. I had not conducted an interview in this manner before where I was the only one asking questions so it was good practice. I think using a table such as the design matrix helps to more easily understand and analyse different comments made so I will try to start incorporating that in any future interviews. I also believe that giving the interviewee a clear structure of what you are planning on discussing at the start is a good idea because it makes them more comfortable which in turn allows them to give more honest feedback. What would you consider improving to learn more from the interviews? My team’s main shortcoming was the fact that we did not have a working prototype to present to the interviewee. To make up for this, we showed our drawings from the design sprint. To learn more, I would have provided a functioning prototype and asked more in-depth questions. Also, interviewing more people with a range of different backgrounds would have given us even more information on how to improve our idea. Design matrix:\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
