nterviews began, we  communicated the purpose and objectives to the participants to ensure they were  well informed and aligned with our intentions.     • Which were you difficulties for finding interviews? What would you  consider would help to reach 5 interviewees?     One of our main challenges was identifying individuals who fit the target audience  criteria for our product design. Reaching out to potential interviewees required  significant time and effort, especially when trying to secure responses from those  who were not already familiar with us or the project.   Leverage Networks: Tapping into pr professional networks to ask for  recommendations could speed up the process.    Clear Communication: Providing potential interviewees with a concise explanation  of the project’s purpose, along with the time commitment and benefits for  participating, could help for the futurr    Incentives: Offering small incentives, might encourage people to participate.   Use Online Platforms: Posting calls for participants on social media or relevant  forums could expand our reach to a broader audience.   Better Schede: Being more accommodating with interview times.     • What are the most suppressive comments from the interview?      There were concerns about usability, about if the  design would be intuitive  enough for all users, especially those less familiar with similar products. These  comments emphasized a possible need to simplify aspects of the design and  product    in a moment of the interview, it was expressed uncertainty about whether the  product would stand out in a competitive market, suggesting we need to refine our  innovative value proposition.     • Which were the observations that generated the mos discussion or took  the longest time?     One key concern was usability. Some participants questioned whether the design  would be intuitive enough for all users, especially those less familiar with similar  products. This feedback suggested a need to simplify certain aspects of the  design.   Another notable comment was about the product\'s market relevance. At one  point, participants expressed uncertainty about whether the product would stand  out in a competitive market, emphasizing the importance of refining our unique  value proposition.  These comments , were valuable in helping us identify areas of  improvement.        • How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your  projects, initiatives, or jobs?     To effectively use the interview technique in future projects, I would start  defining  the objective of the interview and identifying the key aspects of information I need  to gather. This helps to encourage detailed and more extense responses. Id also  research the background of participants to personalize the approach and make the  conversation m ore intersenting to them.      I would alsi focus on improving my listening skills and maintaining a neutral stance  during interviews to avoid influencing the interview.         What do you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the  interviews?     -  Make more  specific questions to avoid vague answers, ensuring they align  directly with the project goals.   - Use follow up questions more effectively to explore deeper thoughts and  motivations, especially when participants provide short answers.   - Improve recording techniques to ensure no detail is missed during analysis.   - Aim for a more diverse field of interviewees to capture a much biggerr range of  perspectives and experiences.   - After the interviews, identify areas of improvement with the team and discuss  areas where we might have missed opportunities for deeper learning.\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'cl