ews </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** The argument incorporates at least 3 interviews. 2. **8-9:** The argument includes less o equal than 2 interviews. 3. **5-7:** The argument includes less or equal than 1 interviews. 4. **2-4:** There is no interviews identified. 5. **1:** There is a general approach to talking about interviews of any kind. 6. **0:** No interviews or evidence of such are provided. </Heuristics> <Category> Syntax </Category>  <Heuristics> 1. **10:** Sentence structure is varied and enhances the readability and engagement of the writing. No syntax errors are present. 2. **8-9:** Sentence structure is clear and varied but includes minor awkwardness or redundancy. 3. **6-7:** Sentence structure is functional but lacks variety or includes some awkward constructions. 4. **4-5:** Sentence structure is often repetitive or overly simplistic, reducing engagement and clarity. 5. **1-3:** Sentence structure is confusing or poorly constructed, with significant redundancy or awkwardness. 6. **0:** Sentence structure is entirely disorganized, making the writing unreadable. </Heuristics> Application of Use of Evidence Scoring Example: Score 0: No sources or real-world examples. Score 5: One credible interview is cited but no project referred (e.g., referencing user names nor application to the contexts). Score 10: Includes three interviews (e.g., interviewees names cited) for the projet, such as citing a project on sports related industries or entities. This detailed breakdown ensures consistency in scoring and facilitates improvement by identifying specific areas of weakness in an essay. Let me know if you need further clarifications or adjustments!`\n\nStudent\'s Solution:\nConsumer Market Research. – Interview insights Level-Up Team members Anaïs Venkatasawmy, Bautista Palma, Anastasiia Lemishko, Sole Ansaldo, Paula Carrasco 17/01/2025 How did you prepare the environment for the interviews? And, the same for the team analysis of the observations? On January 7, 2025, our team representative held an online interview to make it easy for everyone to attend despite the holiday season, because we couldn’t find a time slot due to holidays and family gathering. We used Zoom, this platform was chosen because it’s simple to use and reliable for remote meetings and the interface is simple. We were trying to not make the person that is being interviewed uncomfortable, so the only one interviewer was Anaïs. Before the interview, we worked together to prepare. We have tried to look at this from different perspective and try to imagine how is it going to look for a person that has no idea on how the app works. We made sure the questions were clear and focused on what we wanted to learn. We also divided the interview into three parts: 1.A quick introduction to learn about the person’s experience and challenges as an amateur player, to understand if they are the right choice for this interview, understand if they have ever faced any troubles in that area. 2. A walkthrough of our prototype explained in pictures to show how users can register and explore the site’s features. It was a bit tricky as we didn’t have a real prototype, so the complete understanding of the idea was indented to be created by pictures we already had. 3. A feedback session where the person could share their thoughts and ideas and give criticism. To make the environment comfortable, we picked a quiet time and set up the meeting with a clear agenda. During the session, one team member led the conversation, welcoming the participant and explaining how the interview would go. The rest of the team focused on taking notes and paying attention to their reactions and feedback. As we are respecting privacy, we are not going to mention name of the person that participated in the interview. After the interview, we had a team meeting to review what we observed. We started by looking at our notes individually, then shared our thoughts as a group. To organize the feedback, we used a simple list to highlight the main strengths, areas to improve, and any surprising insights. This approach helped us clearly see what worked and what needed to change in our vision of prototype. What were the difficulties you faced when looking for interviewees? And What do you think would help when trying to reach 5 interviewees? Finding interviewees for the project was challenging, especially because it was the holiday season. People were occupied with finals, work deadlines, school commitments, and holiday activities, leaving little room for extra participation in interviews. Many of the potential participants we reached out to were unavailable or unable to commit to a specific time. Scheduling became a significant hurdle as we tried to balance their availability with our own. Personally, I found it particularly difficult because I am not very familiar with women’s football. This made it harder for me to fully understand the challenges amateur players face when looking for an agent. Without that deeper connection to the subject, it was a challenge to predict what aspects of the platform might resonate most with potential users or what specific questions would help us uncover their needs. To secure interviews and reach a goal of five participants, I think it’s essential to expand networking efforts significantly. Building connections in the right circles, such as amateur football communities, online groups, or social media platforms, could make it easier to identify and reach potential interviewees. Joining forums or groups specifically dedicated to women’s football could also help establish trust and create opportunities to engage with more people. Another strategy would be to leverage existing networks, such as reaching out to sports organizations, local football clubs, or even university teams. Collaborating with people who are already involved in the amateur sports scene could help us find participants who fit the target audience. Starting this process earlier and over-recruiting could also help ensure that we have enough participants, even if some drop out. What are the most suppressive comments from the interview? One of the most striking comments from the interview was about the balance between simplicity and the need for detailed data entry. While users appreciate straightforward and easy-to-use platforms, the current registration process felt too time-consuming and overwhelming. The participant mentioned that filling out all the information to properly showcase themselves as an athlete was taking too long, which could discourage potential users from completing the process.But, having a comprehensive profile is essential for connecting players with agents effectively. This creates a tension between the need for detailed data and maintaining a smooth user experience. Which were the observations that generated the most discussion or took the longest time? The observation that generated the most discussion was  as the registration process and a tracking button. We had a debate about whether the process should prioritize being quick and user-friendly or focus on collecting detailed information to create comprehensive player profiles. One side was more on the side of simplicity, arguing that a lengthy or complex process could deter users from completing it. On the other hand, we recognized that agents need detailed data to assess players effectively. Another point was the “Track” button.It wasn’t as intuitive as we thought. The interviewee didn’t immediately understand what this button led to. How would you prepare yourself to use the interview technique in your projects, initiatives or jobs? And, what you consider improving for obtaining more learning from the interviews. The interview technique is an interesting but for me I would not prioritize it in the future. One of the main drawbacks is that it doesn’t always provide a wide range of opinions, as the feedback is limited to the experiences and perspectives of a small number of participants. To prepare for using the interview technique, I think the most important step is to approach it with an open mind. It’s crucial to avoid categorizing answers as simply “good” or “bad.” Instead, try to view the feedback through the perspective of the user. This means understanding their challenges and preferences without being prejudiced or judgmental. DESIGN MATRIX\n\nPlease provide your response in valid JSON format:\n{\n    "analysis": "Your analysis here",\n    "criterion_scores": {\n        "criterion1": "score",\n        "criterion2": "score"\n    },\n    "total_score": "final_score",\n    "feedback": "Your feedback here"\n}\n\n\nAssistant:'}], 'model': 'claude-3-haiku-20240307', 'temperature': 0.0}}
